STRATIFIED ABSTRACT SET THEORY (SAST)
A Pure Formal Framework for Abstraction, Definability, and Ideals
1. The Stratified Universe
The universe U is stratified into levels:

text
U = ⋃_{n ∈ ℤ} L_n
Where each L_n is a level of abstraction.

Axiom 1: Stratification

text
∀x ∈ U, ∃! n ∈ ℤ such that x ∈ L_n
2. The Levels
Level	Name	Description
L_0	Sensory	Raw experience, pre-formal
L_1	First-Order	Patterns extracted from sensory
L_2	Second-Order	Concepts about concepts
L_3	Third-Order	Systems of concepts
L_n	n-th Order	Higher abstractions
L_∞	Ideals	Approachable, unattainable
Axiom 2: Ordering

text
∀n > m, L_n is an abstraction of L_m
3. The Abstraction Mapping
Definition 1: Abstraction

text
α: L_n → L_{n+1}
α is many-to-one
Definition 2: Realization

text
τ: L_{n+1} → P(L_n)
τ(s) = {x ∈ L_n | α(x) = s}
τ is one-to-many
Definition 3: Abstraction Composition

text
α^k(x) = α(α^{k-1}(x))
α^0(x) = x
4. The Core Axioms
Axiom 3: First-Order Primacy

text
∀x ∈ L_1, ∀y ∈ L_n (n > 1), Priority(x) > Priority(y)
Axiom 4: Loss of Definability

text
∀n > 1, Definability(L_n) < Definability(L_{n-1})
Axiom 5: Definability Function

text
Definability(L_n) = 1 / (1 + e^{λn})
where λ > 0 is the rate of loss.

Axiom 6: Ideals Unattainability

text
∀i ∈ L_∞, ¬∃x ∈ U such that x = i
∀i ∈ L_∞, ∀ε > 0, ∃x ∈ U such that d(x, i) < ε
Axiom 7: Ideals Approachability

text
∀i ∈ L_∞, Approach(i) = {x ∈ U | d(x, i) < ε}
5. Information Theory
Definition 4: Entropy of a Level

text
H(L_n) = -∑_{x ∈ L_n} P(x) log P(x)
Theorem 1: Entropy Increases with Abstraction

text
∀n > 0, H(L_n) > H(L_{n-1})
Proof:

Step	Statement	Justification
1	α: L_n → L_{n+1} is many-to-one	Definition 1
2	Many-to-one mapping loses distinguishability	Set theory
3	Loss of distinguishability increases entropy	Shannon entropy
4	Therefore, H(L_{n+1}) > H(L_n)	From 1-3
6. Formal Operations
Operation 1: Abstraction

text
α(x) = y, where x ∈ L_n, y ∈ L_{n+1}
Operation 2: Realization

text
τ(y) = {x ∈ L_n | α(x) = y}
Operation 3: Level Projection

text
π_n(x) = the projection of x onto L_n
Operation 4: Ideal Approach

text
A(i, ε) = {x ∈ U | d(x, i) < ε}
7. The Binary (For Reference)
Definition 5: The Binary

text
B = {0, 1}
Observation 1: Binary is Artificial

text
The binary is not observed in L_0.
It is introduced at L_1 as a survival abstraction.
It serves as a reference tool.
8. Theorems
Theorem 2: First-Order Primacy

text
∀x ∈ L_1, Value(x) > Value(y) for all y ∈ L_n (n > 1)
Proof: Direct from Axiom 3.

Theorem 3: Definability Decay

text
lim_{n→∞} Definability(L_n) = 0
Proof: From Definability(L_n) = 1 / (1 + e^{λn}), as n → ∞, D → 0.

Theorem 4: Ideals Limit

text
lim_{ε→0} A(i, ε) = ∅
Proof: Ideals are unattainable; the limit point is not in U.

Theorem 5: Information Loss

text
lim_{n→∞} H(L_n) = ∞
Proof: Entropy increases with each abstraction; higher abstractions contain less information.

Theorem 6: Reference Property

text
∀x ∈ L_n (n > 1), x is a reference to L_{n-1}
Proof: All abstractions reference their source level.

9. The Complete Formal System
text
UNIVERSE: U = ⋃_{n∈ℤ} L_n
LEVELS: L_n for n ∈ ℤ
ABSTRUCTION: α: L_n → L_{n+1}, many-to-one
REALIZATION: τ: L_{n+1} → P(L_n), one-to-many
ENTROPY: H(L_n) = -∑_{x∈L_n} P(x) log P(x)
DEFINABILITY: D(L_n) = 1 / (1 + e^{λn})
IDEALS: L_∞, approachable, unattainable
BINARY: B = {0, 1}, for reference only
PRIORITY: L_1 > L_n for n > 1
